.TH sparsemap 3 "2026-06-03" "sparsemap 2.3.0" "Sparsemap Library"
.SH NAME
sm_create, sm_free, sm_add, sm_contains, sm_remove, sm_cardinality,
sm_union, sm_intersection, sm_difference \- sparse compressed bitmap
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <sparsemap/sm.h>
.PP
Lifecycle:
.RS
.BI "sparsemap_t *sm_create(size_t " size );
.BI "sparsemap_t *sm_copy(const sparsemap_t *" other );
.BI "sparsemap_t *sm_owned_copy(const sparsemap_t *" map );
.BI "sparsemap_t *sm_wrap(uint8_t *" data ", size_t " size );
.BI "void sm_init(sparsemap_t *" map ", uint8_t *" data ", size_t " size );
.BI "void sm_open(sparsemap_t *" map ", uint8_t *" data ", size_t " size );
.BI "void sm_clear(sparsemap_t *" map );
.BI "void sm_free(sparsemap_t *" map );
.BI "sparsemap_t *sm_set_data_size(sparsemap_t *" map ", uint8_t *" data ", size_t " size );
.RE
.PP
Single-bit:
.RS
.BI "bool sm_contains(sparsemap_t *" map ", uint64_t " idx );
.BI "uint64_t sm_add(sparsemap_t *" map ", uint64_t " idx );
.BI "uint64_t sm_remove(sparsemap_t *" map ", uint64_t " idx );
.BI "uint64_t sm_assign(sparsemap_t *" map ", uint64_t " idx ", bool " value );
.RE
.PP
Aggregates and queries:
.RS
.BI "size_t sm_cardinality(sparsemap_t *" map );
.BI "uint64_t sm_minimum(const sparsemap_t *" map );
.BI "uint64_t sm_maximum(const sparsemap_t *" map );
.BI "size_t sm_rank(sparsemap_t *" map ", uint64_t " x ", uint64_t " y ", bool " value );
.BI "uint64_t sm_select(sparsemap_t *" map ", uint64_t " n ", bool " value );
.BI "uint64_t sm_span(sparsemap_t *" map ", uint64_t " start ", size_t " len ", bool " value );
.RE
.PP
Bulk:
.RS
.BI "sparsemap_t *sm_union(const sparsemap_t *" a ", const sparsemap_t *" b );
.BI "sparsemap_t *sm_intersection(const sparsemap_t *" a ", const sparsemap_t *" b );
.BI "sparsemap_t *sm_difference(const sparsemap_t *" a ", const sparsemap_t *" b );
.BI "uint64_t sm_split(sparsemap_t *" map ", uint64_t " idx ", sparsemap_t *" other );
.BI "sparsemap_t *sm_offset(const sparsemap_t *" map ", ssize_t " offset );
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
Sparsemap is a mutable, resizable, run-length-encoded compressed
bitmap.  Best case stores 16 KB of consecutive set bits in 8 bytes;
worst case is uncompressed (2048 bits + 8 bytes overhead per chunk).
See
.BR sparsemap (7)
for the architecture and the project's
.IR docs/API.md
for the full reference.
.PP
The library is single-threaded.  Concurrent reads of an immutable map
are safe; any writer must hold an external lock.
.SH ALLOCATION LINEAGE
Every sparsemap has an internal allocation-lineage tag controlling
how its data buffer can be resized and freed.  Three lineages exist:
.TP
.B SM_OWNED_CONTIGUOUS
.IR sm_create (),
.IR sm_copy (),
.IR sm_owned_copy ().
The struct and buffer are one heap block.  Free with
.IR sm_free ()
or libc
.IR free ().
.TP
.B SM_WRAPPED
.IR sm_wrap (),
.IR sm_init (),
.IR sm_open ().
The library does not own the data buffer.  Free with
.IR sm_free ()
(frees the struct only); the caller frees their buffer
independently.
.TP
.B SM_OWNED_SPLIT
Produced when
.IR sm_set_data_size ()
grows a wrapped map: the library allocates a fresh buffer.  Must be
freed with
.IR sm_free ()
\(em libc
.IR free ()
will leak the buffer.
.PP
.IR sm_set_data_size (
.I map
, NULL,
.I new_size
)
never silently no-ops: it grows the buffer (relocating the map for the
contiguous lineage, or promoting a wrapped map to owned-split) or
returns
.B NULL
on allocation failure.
.SH ERRORS
Mutating functions return
.B SM_IDX_MAX
and set
.I errno
to
.B ENOSPC
when the backing buffer is full.  The caller can grow with
.IR sm_set_data_size ()
and retry.  Allocation functions return
.B NULL
on allocation failure.
.SH EMBEDDING
The library is two files,
.I sm.h
and
.IR sm.c ,
so it can be vendored by copying them into another source tree.  To
let two independently-vendored copies coexist in one binary, define
.B SM_PREFIX
before including the header:
.PP
.RS
.nf
.B #define SM_PREFIX myapp_
.B #include <sparsemap/sm.h>
.fi
.RE
.PP
Every public function and type then carries the prefix
.RB ( sm_create
becomes
.IR myapp_sm_create ,
.B sparsemap_t
becomes
.IR myapp_sparsemap_t ).
Compile-time macros
.RB ( SM_IDX_MAX ,
the
.B SM_VERSION_*
values, the enum constants) are not renamed, and the serialized wire
format is unchanged.
.PP
The wire format produced by
.IR sm_serialize ()
and read by
.IR sm_deserialize ()/ sm_open ()
is stable across releases within a major version.  The in-memory
.B struct sparsemap
layout is not stable: always allocate via the constructors and treat
the type as opaque.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR sparsemap (7)
